{"type":"message","id":"f8410021","parentId":"c504f69e","timestamp":"2026-05-04T05:09:47.857Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user asks to run the crypto news pipeline. According to SOUL.md, we must follow steps: research, write, create image, publish, final report.\n\nWe need to ensure proper <thinking> blocks before each step. Must use rm -f the session files, run openclaw agent commands, capture outputs.\n\nWe need to store RESEARCH_JSON, probably as a variable. Then article output saved at /tmp/crypto-article.md etc. Need to extract image generation result path? The creator agent command will generate image and presumably output path? The SOUL says: \"If the creator returned a valid file path (not IMAGE_FAILED)\" so the creator agent output likely contains the path. We'll need to capture its output.\n\nAlso need to get the article title? The writer might include a title in the article file. So for image generation we need ARTICLE_TITLE variable from article? We can parse first line maybe.\n\nWe need to process steps sequentially, informing user after each.\n\nImplement: Step 1 - exec command to remove session file, then run openclaw agent ... --deliver, capture output to a file or variable. Use exec and capture output.\n\nWe should probably write RESEARCH_JSON to a file for later use. The command is: openclaw agent --agent researcher --message \"... --deliver\". We need to capture output; use exec with command redirect > /tmp/research.json. Then read file for extraction later.\n\nStep 2 - similar: rm sessions, run writer agent, redirect > /tmp/crypto-article.md, then run sed to strip thinking blocks, then check for Word Count presence, maybe re-run.\n\nStep 3 - extract fields from RESEARCH_JSON: topic_theme, combined_key_facts first 2 items, primary crypto asset. We'll need to parse JSON; could use jq via exec. Yes.\n\nAssume RESEARCH_JSON format includes keys: topic_theme, combined_key_facts (array), primary_asset (string). We'll use jq.\n\nThen set env vars or extract via exec command substitution; we can do via exec and capture outputs.\n\nStep 4 - run creator agent; capture output (maybe a path). Then decide if image succeeded.\n\nStep 5 - add image to article if present, convert with pandoc, then run publisher agent, capture JSON output, extract webViewLink.\n\nNeed to ensure we use <thinking> blocks before each exec to confirm.\n\nLet's implement sequentially using exec commands. We'll need to read files to check conditions.\n\nWe'll report status to user after each step.\n\nPotential issues: if any step fails, we need to handle fallback. For brevity, we can assume success. But we should check for failures and report.\n\nWe'll use exec with background false default, capture output.\n\nLet's start with step1.\n\n<thinking>\nStep 1: Research.\nWe need to clear researcher session JSON, run openclaw agent researcher with specific message, deliver output to RESEARCH_JSON file.\nCommand:\nrm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/researcher/sessions/sessions.json && openclaw agent --agent researcher --message \"Follow your SOUL to cross-reference multiple RSS feeds, find the single biggest news event, and extract deep facts. Return the aggregated Single-Topic JSON.\" --deliver > /tmp/research.json\nWe will execute via exec and capture output. Ensure command runs fully. \n</thinking>","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"<thinking>\nStep 1: Research.\nWe need to clear researcher session JSON, run openclaw agent researcher with specific message, deliver output to RESEARCH_JSON file.\nCommand:\nrm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/researcher/sessions/sessions.json && openclaw agent --agent researcher --message \"Follow your SOUL to cross-reference multiple RSS feeds, find the single biggest news event, and extract deep facts. Return the aggregated Single-Topic JSON.\" --deliver > /tmp/research.json\nWe will execute via exec and capture output. Ensure command runs fully. \n</thinking>"}],"api